 </section>

 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; Figure 1 and its quiz cover holding entries.</p>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> You keep drifting off altitude and are watching the altimeter more and more. What is wrong, and what is the fix?</summary><p>Fixation. The attitude is what is taking you off altitude, so go back to the hub-and-spoke crosscheck from the attitude indicator, set a small pitch correction, and let the altimeter catch up.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Why trim only after setting the attitude?</summary><p>Trim relieves control pressure for an attitude you have already set; flying the aircraft with trim is not attitude control.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> In a long, level turn in cloud you look down at a chart. Suddenly you feel the aircraft rolling in another axis. What is it?</summary><p>The Coriolis illusion, from an abrupt head movement in a prolonged constant-rate turn. Go to the instruments and avoid sudden head movements in long turns.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> In a steady descending turn the instruments show altitude falling but you feel wings level. What will the disoriented pilot do?</summary><p>Pull back, tightening the spiral and losing more altitude: the graveyard spiral. Level the wings on the attitude indicator first.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> The attitude indicator shows a steep dive, but the airspeed and altimeter are steady. What do you do?</summary><p>Verify before recovering: the control and performance instruments disagree, so check the standby or other attitude indicator. If in doubt about the attitude indicator, use the attitude-indicator-inoperative procedure.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Fixed wing, diving, inverted. When can you pull?</summary><p>Not until under 90° of bank: roll to wings level and upright while adjusting power or drag, then pull to level flight.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> The plate gives no climb gradient. What must an Air Force pilot climb at, and when may they turn?</summary><p>At least 200 ft per nautical mile with all engines operating, and no turn until 400 ft above the departure end unless the procedure requires an early turn.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> Holding at 16,000 ft away from a military field, with no published speed. What is the maximum speed, and how long is the inbound leg?</summary><p>265 KIAS, and 1½ minutes inbound, because the hold is above 14,000 ft.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> Inbound you need 5° of right drift correction. What do you hold outbound?</summary><p>15° left: three times the inbound correction, the other way.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> On a non-precision approach you see the runway at the MDA, before the VDP. May you descend?</summary><p>Not yet: do not leave the MDA before the VDP, even with the runway in sight. At or after it, with the runway environment in sight and in position to land, yes.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> On an ILS you are two dots low at 500 ft above localizer minimums. Can you continue?</summary><p>Not below localizer minimums while more than one dot low. If you recapture the glide slope within one dot low or two dots high, you may continue to the DA.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 7</span> On a PAR final you hear nothing for five seconds. What do you do?</summary><p>Call the controller. If you cannot reestablish contact and cannot stay VFR, fly your backup approach.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 7</span> You decide to go missed a mile before the MAP. Can you turn right away?</summary><p>No: unless ATC clears otherwise, fly the approach to the MAP at or above the DA or MDA before turning.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 8</span> The plate shows an emergency safe altitude. What does it give you?</summary><p>At least 1,000 ft of obstacle clearance (2,000 ft in designated mountainous areas) within 100 NM of the facility, for emergency use; not navigation aid reception.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 8</span> Radio failure in cloud, assigned 6,000 ft, told to expect 8,000 ft at the next fix, on a segment where the minimum is 5,000 ft. What altitude?</summary><p>6,000 ft to the fix, then 8,000 ft: the highest of the assigned, minimum and expected altitudes for each segment.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military source</h3>
 <p id="source-afman"><strong>[1] US Air Force, AFMAN 11-217 Volume 1, Instrument Flight Procedures, 22 October 2010.</strong> <a href="https://www.code7700.com/pdfs/usaf/afman11-217v1.pdf">Read on code7700.com's copy</a>, 2 November 2026, by printed page: chapter 1 (pp. 12–15), turns and unusual attitudes (pp. 19–20, 26–29), approach plates (pp. 103–106), departures (pp. 127–128), holding (pp. 151–159), high-altitude approaches (pp. 171–174), final approach, ILS and radar (pp. 206–210, 219–222), missed approach (pp. 249–250). Superseded by the consolidated AFMAN 11-217 of 10 June 2019, which could not be retrieved; numbers may have changed.</p>
 <h3>Supplementary FAA sources</h3>
 <p id="source-aim5"><strong>[2] FAA, Aeronautical Information Manual, chapter 5, section 3: En Route Procedures.</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap5_section_3.html">faa.gov</a>, read 2 November 2026: ¶5-3-8, Holding.</p>
 <p id="source-aim6"><strong>[3] FAA, Aeronautical Information Manual, chapter 6, section 4: Two-way Radio Communications Failure.</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap6_section_4.html">faa.gov</a>, read 2 November 2026: ¶6-4-1, quoting 14 CFR 91.185.</p>
 <p id="source-aim8"><strong>[4] FAA, Aeronautical Information Manual, chapter 8, section 1: Fitness for Flight.</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap8_section_1.html">faa.gov</a>, read 2 November 2026: ¶8-1-5, Illusions in Flight.</p>
 <h3>Sim source</h3>
 <p id="source-a10"><strong>[5] Eagle Dynamics, DCS: A-10C II Tank Killer Flight Manual (English), July 2023.</strong> <a href="https://www.digitalcombatsimulator.com/en/downloads/documentation/dcs-warthog_2_flight_manual_en/">Download page</a>, read 2 November 2026, pp. 573–574 and 600–603. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/instrument-flight-evidence.md">instrument flight evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figure 1, its quiz and the opening sortie are original, invented examples, not reproductions of any publication's figure.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for fixed- and rotary-wing pilots of every service. Everything outside the sim panel is from the Air Force manual or the FAA's manual. The Navy's instrument flight training instructions and NATOPS instrument flight manual, and the Army's instrument publications, could not be read for this revision, so no Navy, Marine Corps or Army rules are included beyond the Air Force manual's Navy airfield holding speed. Your service's rules, the aircraft's flight manual and current FLIP govern.</p>
